[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The compact shell"]

{ Volume 1: The King's Indian from First Principles. Black lets White occupy the center because the pawns can become targets and because development is completed without fixing the central structure too soon. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 { Black plan: Castle, watch e5 and c5, and keep every piece connected to a future break. White plan: White wants to stabilize e4 and d4, finish development, and use the extra space without creating hooks. Decision: Decide whether the next useful act is ...e5, ...c5, or a preparation that makes one break sound. Warning: Copying the setup without challenging the center leaves Black cramped for no compensation. Memory: The shell is only the launch platform; the break is the opening. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "1.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The classical closed center"]

{ Volume 1: The King's Indian from First Principles. After d5 the center becomes a directional map: White normally expands on the queenside while Black prepares kingside play. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 { Black plan: Re-route the f6-knight, prepare ...f5, and count the race before pushing pawns. White plan: White seeks c5, b4, and pressure against c7 or d6. Decision: Compare the speed of ...f5 with White's c5 break and change plans if the center can reopen. Warning: A kingside pawn storm is unsound when White can open the center against Black's king first. Memory: Closed chains point the wings, but tempi decide the race. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "1.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The flank challenge with c5"]

{ Volume 1: The King's Indian from First Principles. The ...c5 system attacks d4 from the flank and often produces Benoni-like structures rather than a locked Mar del Plata. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 c5 7. d5 e6 8. O-O exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 { Black plan: Pressure e4 and d5, use the e-file, and seek ...b5 or ...Nbd7 according to White's setup. White plan: White uses the space advantage, e4-e5 possibilities, and queenside majority. Decision: Once ...c5 and ...e6 change the skeleton, stop using automatic closed-center attacking rules. Warning: Playing ...f5 by habit can weaken e6 and the king before the pieces are ready. Memory: Name the resulting structure before choosing the plan. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "2.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The e5 lock"]

{ Volume 2: Central Breaks and Structure Recognition. The ...e5 contact invites d5, after which Black accepts less space in return for a clear kingside break and useful knight routes. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 { Black plan: Use ...Nd7, ...f5, and often ...Nf6 or ...f4 while keeping c7 and d6 under control. White plan: White expands with c5 and b4 and may trade on d6. Decision: Choose the moment of ...f5 by checking whether White can answer with exf5, c5, or a central opening. Warning: Pushing ...f5 before the pieces can use the opened files produces weaknesses rather than an attack. Memory: ...e5 asks d5 a question; ...f5 answers the closed center. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "2.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The c5 transformation"]

{ Volume 2: Central Breaks and Structure Recognition. The paired breaks ...c5 and ...e6 undermine White's center and can exchange space for targets and active lines. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 c5 7. d5 e6 8. O-O exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 { Black plan: Attack e4, contest the e-file, and prepare queenside play rather than forcing a kingside race. White plan: White may advance e5, defend d5, or use b4 to gain space. Decision: Calculate each central capture because the order determines who owns the e-file and whether d5 is weak or protected. Warning: Calling every g6-Bg7 position a King's Indian can hide a full transition to Benoni logic. Memory: The pawn skeleton outranks the opening name. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "2.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The delayed c6 lever"]

{ Volume 2: Central Breaks and Structure Recognition. ...c6 can challenge d5, prepare ...Nc5, or support ...d5 without immediately fixing the queenside. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Na6 8. Re1 c6 9. Bf1 exd4 10. Nxd4 Re8 { Black plan: Keep the center flexible, pressure e4, and use the e-file after exchanges. White plan: White may hold the center, play d5, or exploit the slower knight on a6. Decision: Ask whether ...c6 prepares ...d5, invites d5, or merely creates a target before committing. Warning: A preparation move is wasted if the intended break never becomes possible. Memory: Every pawn move must name the transformation it prepares. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
