[Round "3.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Main-line entry"]

{ Volume 3: The Classical System Repertoire Map. This move order reaches the main closed center with maximum pressure on d4 and a clear division of wings. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 { Black plan: Prepare the knight route and ...f5 while monitoring White's c5 and b4 timing. White plan: White chooses among Ne1, Nd2, b4, Bg5, or a4 to accelerate queenside play. Decision: On move nine identify White's setup before choosing ...Nd7, ...Nh5, ...a5, or another preparation. Warning: Treating all ninth moves alike loses vital tempi in a race. Memory: The branch is identified by White's first queenside commitment. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "3.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The quieter Orthodox route"]

{ Volume 3: The Classical System Repertoire Map. ...Nbd7 avoids an immediate commitment of the queen knight and supports ...c6, though it gives different pressure than ...Nc6. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Re1 c6 9. Bf1 Re8 10. Rb1 a6 { Black plan: Prepare a central challenge and keep the rook useful on e8. White plan: White gains space, supports b4, and may keep the center fluid. Decision: Compare the benefits of ...Nc6 pressure with ...Nbd7 flexibility before selecting a repertoire branch. Warning: Mixing the plans can place both knights on passive squares. Memory: A move order is a package of piece routes, not a cosmetic choice. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "3.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Gligoric's Be3 question"]

{ Volume 3: The Classical System Repertoire Map. White develops the bishop before castling and asks Black to reveal the kingside structure. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh6 { Black plan: Gain time on the bishop, control f4, and decide whether the space gained by ...g5 is worth the dark-square cost. White plan: White wants d5, Qd2, and queenside castling or a stable central edge. Decision: Before ...g5 calculate whether the center is closed enough and whether h5 becomes a weakness. Warning: Chasing the bishop without central safety can expose Black's own king. Memory: A tempo gain is real only when the pawn weaknesses are affordable. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "4.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The knight rerouting machine"]

{ Volume 4: Mar del Plata - Black's Attacking Engine. The f6-knight leaves the f-pawn free, then returns after ...f5 to support ...f4 and kingside entry squares. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 { Black plan: Coordinate ...f5, ...Nf6, ...f4, and a later ...g5 without abandoning the queenside. White plan: White prepares c5, b4, and pressure on d6 while using f3 to hold e4. Decision: Count whether the knight route loses too many tempi compared with White's c5 break. Warning: Automatic maneuvering can be too slow if White opens the queenside immediately. Memory: The knight moves twice because each square serves a different phase. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "4.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The pawn-chain attack"]

{ Volume 4: Mar del Plata - Black's Attacking Engine. Black fixes space with ...f4 and ...g5, then uses ...h5 to create contact rather than merely accumulating pawns. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 13. Be1 g5 14. g4 Ng6 15. h3 h5 { Black plan: Open a file or diagonal only when queen, rook, bishop, and knight can use it. White plan: White races with c5 and b4 or closes the kingside with g4 and h3. Decision: Decide whether to capture, advance, or maintain tension on g4 and h5 by calculating the opened lines. Warning: A pawn storm with no line-opening mechanism becomes an overextended wall. Memory: Pawns create contact; pieces deliver the attack. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "4.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The two-wing clock"]

{ Volume 4: Mar del Plata - Black's Attacking Engine. White's c5 break and Black's ...f5-f4 advance are simultaneous plans whose value depends on arrival time and resulting access. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. c5 Nf6 12. f3 f4 13. b4 g5 14. cxd6 cxd6 { Black plan: Continue the kingside attack only if c7 and d6 can survive long enough. White plan: White opens the c-file, attacks d6, and may create a queenside passer. Decision: Count forcing moves on both wings instead of comparing how far the pawns have advanced. Warning: Ignoring a queenside breakthrough because the king is far away can lose material before the attack matures. Memory: The faster plan is the one with forcing moves, not the one with more space. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "5.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Nh5 response"]

{ Volume 5: The Bayonet Attack and Queenside Expansion. ...Nh5 clears the f-pawn, eyes f4, and keeps the e7-knight available while White has committed to b4. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. Re1 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. Bf3 c6 { Black plan: Challenge with ...f5 and use ...c6 when it strikes White's center or b5 support. White plan: White wants c5, a4, and a queenside breakthrough before Black organizes. Decision: Test whether ...f5 or ...c6 must come first in the exact move order. Warning: Repeating a memorized order can allow e5 or Ne6 tactics. Memory: Against the Bayonet, move order is part of the defense. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "5.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The capture on f5"]

{ Volume 5: The Bayonet Attack and Queenside Expansion. White can clarify f5 to slow the pawn roller, but the recapture activates Black's dark bishop and changes e4 pressure. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. g3 f5 11. exf5 Bxf5 12. Ng5 Nf6 { Black plan: Use the active bishop, contest e4, and avoid overextending before development catches up. White plan: White seeks g4, Ne6, or queenside play while the bishop is exposed. Decision: Compare ...Bxf5 with other recaptures by activity, king safety, and control of e4. Warning: A visually active bishop can become a tempo target if Black has no follow-up. Memory: Evaluate the recapture by the next two useful moves. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "5.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The a-file transformation"]

{ Volume 5: The Bayonet Attack and Queenside Expansion. ...a5 attacks the base of White's expansion and can open the a-file before c5 becomes permanent. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. c5 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 a5 13. bxa5 Rxa5 { Black plan: Use the rook actively and pressure c5 or a2 while keeping the kingside break alive. White plan: White wants to stabilize c5, use Nb5, and gain access to d6. Decision: Calculate whether opening the a-file creates real entry squares or merely trades a pawn weakness. Warning: Opening a file is pointless if only the opponent can enter it. Memory: A flank break is justified by the line it opens for a piece. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "6.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Petrosian's early d5"]

{ Volume 6: Petrosian, Gligoric, and Orthodox Alternatives. White closes the center before castling and uses Bg5 to reduce Black's easy kingside expansion. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. d5 a5 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Na6 10. Nd2 Qe8 { Black plan: Gain queenside restraint with ...a5, reroute through a6, and prepare ...Nh7 or ...Nd7 with patience. White plan: White aims for g4, h4, and queenside play while limiting ...f5. Decision: Decide whether the first counterplay belongs on f5, c6, or the queenside based on White's king. Warning: Forcing a stock attack before White castles can send play toward the wrong wing. Memory: When the king is uncommitted, keep the attack flexible. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "6.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Gligoric bishop"]

{ Volume 6: Petrosian, Gligoric, and Orthodox Alternatives. Be3 develops naturally and discourages some immediate plans, so Black uses piece attacks to gain time. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. Be3 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh6 { Black plan: Make the bishop's retreat meaningful by controlling f4 and preparing a central or kingside expansion. White plan: White wants d5, Qd2, and a stable route for the king. Decision: Check whether each pawn push attacks a piece and improves a future break. Warning: Pawn moves made only to chase can create permanent holes. Memory: Chase a piece only when the chasing pawn joins the plan. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "6.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Orthodox pressure with c6"]

{ Volume 6: Petrosian, Gligoric, and Orthodox Alternatives. The Orthodox setup keeps central tension and prepares queenside expansion without committing to a closed-center race. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nbd7 8. Re1 c6 9. Bf1 Re8 10. Rb1 a6 { Black plan: Coordinate ...c6, ...b5, and e-file pressure while retaining ...exd4. White plan: White supports b4 and may choose d5 only when it gains something concrete. Decision: Reassess immediately when the center closes; the piece placement then needs a new plan. Warning: Staying in a flexible shell after the position demands a break produces passivity. Memory: Flexibility has value only if you eventually choose. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "7.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The e5 and c6 structure"]

{ Volume 7: The Saemisch System. Black challenges the broad center while White's f3 setup supports e4 and prepares queenside castling. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 c6 8. Qd2 cxd5 9. cxd5 a6 { Black plan: Pressure the center, prepare ...b5 or ...Nh5, and watch the king's destination. White plan: White seeks Qd2, long castling, g4, and a direct attack. Decision: Choose the wing of counterplay only after White reveals the king. Warning: Attacking the queenside automatically can open lines toward your own targets after opposite-side castling. Memory: In the Saemisch, king location determines the meaning of every pawn break. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "7.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The g4 space grab"]

{ Volume 7: The Saemisch System. White uses g4-g5 to restrict f6 and claim kingside space before castling. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 c6 8. g4 cxd5 9. cxd5 h5 10. g5 Nh7 { Black plan: Challenge the base, find squares for the knight, and counter in the center or queenside. White plan: White may follow with h4 and long castling, but the advanced pawns can become targets. Decision: Decide whether ...h5 creates a useful lock or gives White a hook. Warning: Closing the kingside can help White if Black has no counterplay elsewhere. Memory: A restraining pawn move must also create a route for the restricted piece. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "7.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Panno knight route"]

{ Volume 7: The Saemisch System. The Panno setup provokes central expansion and then attacks it with ...c6 while keeping ...e5 available. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 Nc6 7. d5 Ne5 8. f4 Ned7 9. Nf3 c6 { Black plan: Use the knight's retreat to support c5, e5, or b6 and avoid trapping it. White plan: White gains space with d5 and f4 and may advance e5. Decision: Treat the retreat square as part of the plan, not as a concession made without a follow-up. Warning: Provoking pawns is only useful if the resulting targets can be attacked. Memory: A retreat earns its tempo by making the opponent's center less stable. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "8.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The c5 and e6 challenge"]

{ Volume 8: The Four Pawns Attack. White has built maximum space, so Black attacks the chain before development makes it untouchable. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 { Black plan: Pressure e4 and d5, use the e-file, and finish development with tempo. White plan: White may push e5, support the center, or expand on the kingside. Decision: Calculate the central capture order and whether e4-e5 is dangerous. Warning: Waiting allows White to complete development behind a space advantage. Memory: Against four pawns, counterattack the center before admiring it. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "8.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The e5 advance"]

{ Volume 8: The Four Pawns Attack. White's e5 push gains space but also creates tactical contact with the f6-knight and e-file. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 { Black plan: Attack e5, use ...Ng4 or ...Nfd7 routes, and exploit the exposed central king. White plan: White wants to drive the knight and maintain the pawn wedge. Decision: Calculate every forcing response to e5 before choosing a routine developing move. Warning: If Black retreats passively, the center can become a stable attacking platform. Memory: A pawn advance is strongest before contact and weakest immediately after it creates targets. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "8.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Na6 alternative"]

{ Volume 8: The Four Pawns Attack. ...Na6 develops away from the e-pawn's advance and prepares pressure on e4 or c5. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 Na6 7. Be2 e5 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. d5 Nc5 { Black plan: Use the knight actively on c5 and challenge the center with ...c6 or ...c5. White plan: White closes with d5 and tries to preserve space while completing development. Decision: Judge whether the c5 outpost is stable and whether White can b4 the knight. Warning: A knight on the rim is justified only by a concrete route. Memory: Name the destination before choosing the detour. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "9.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The symmetrical bishop battle"]

{ Volume 9: The Fianchetto Variation. Both kings are safe and both bishops influence the long diagonal, so central structure and breaks matter more than a direct pawn storm. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4 c6 { Black plan: Prepare ...c6, ...exd4, or ...Re8 and seek useful queenside expansion. White plan: White wants d5, h3, Be3, and control of the center without weakening the king. Decision: Decide whether closing with ...e5 is active or whether ...c5 gives better pressure. Warning: Copying Mar del Plata kingside play ignores White's fianchetto defense. Memory: Against a fianchetto, attack the center before the shelter. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "9.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The e-file exchange"]

{ Volume 9: The Fianchetto Variation. Exchanging on d4 can expose e4 and create active e-file pressure without conceding the long diagonal. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4 c6 9. h3 exd4 10. Nxd4 Re8 11. Re1 Nc5 { Black plan: Use rooks and knights against e4 while stopping White's queenside expansion. White plan: White centralizes the knight and may use Be3, Qd2, or b4. Decision: Choose ...exd4 only when the opened file and piece activity compensate for releasing tension. Warning: Clarifying the center can solve White's space problem for free. Memory: Release tension only when the open line belongs to you. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "9.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The closed fianchetto center"]

{ Volume 9: The Fianchetto Variation. White closes the center and builds e4, producing a slower battle with queenside and central maneuvering. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. d5 a5 9. e4 Nc5 10. Be3 { Black plan: Restrain b4 with ...a5, use c5 or e7 routes, and search for ...f5 only when supported. White plan: White prepares Ne1-d3, b3, or queenside space while the g2-bishop remains influential. Decision: Check whether ...f5 opens the g2-bishop toward b7 or creates real kingside access. Warning: A thematic break can improve the opponent's best piece. Memory: Ask which bishop benefits before opening a diagonal. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "10.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Averbakh bishop"]

{ Volume 10: Averbakh and Makogonov Systems. Bg5 restrains easy ...e5 and can discourage ...f5, so Black strikes with ...c5 before White consolidates. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Bg5 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. exd5 { Black plan: Challenge d4 and d5, use the e-file, and develop the queen knight according to the structure. White plan: White wants Qd2, h4, and a stable space advantage. Decision: Decide whether the center should be opened before White castles. Warning: A slow setup lets White complete development with every strategic question answered. Memory: When one break is restrained, test the other break immediately. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "10.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Makogonov's h3 and g4"]

{ Volume 10: Averbakh and Makogonov Systems. White prevents ...Ng4 and gains kingside space, but the pawn moves delay other development. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 Na6 8. g4 Nc5 9. f3 a5 { Black plan: Use ...Na6-c5, restrain b4 with ...a5, and target the extended kingside later. White plan: White supports g5, h4, and often queenside castling or a kingside space bind. Decision: Choose counterplay based on the king's final location and the stability of e4. Warning: Reacting to every pawn push can leave Black without a central plan. Memory: Attack the base of the space, not the visible tip. } *

