[Round "11.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Nge2 clamp"]

{ Volume 11: Early e4 Deviations. Nge2-g3 supports f5 and h5 ideas while leaving the f-pawn free, but the knight can become a target. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nge2 O-O 6. Ng3 e5 7. d5 h5 8. Bg5 Qe8 { Black plan: Use ...h5 and queen coordination to limit White's kingside space while preserving central play. White plan: White seeks h4, Be2, and a slow bind. Decision: Judge ...h5 by whether it fixes a target or merely creates one on g6. Warning: Flank prevention without central activity can become self-restraint. Memory: A useful hook is one your pieces can attack. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "12.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "London structure"]

{ Volume 12: Anti-King's Indian Systems. Without c4 and e4, White gives Black more freedom to prepare ...e5 and to challenge the center directly. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O 5. Be2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. h3 Qe8 { Black plan: Use ...e5, ...Nh5, or ...c5 according to White's support of d4 and the bishop's retreat squares. White plan: White wants a stable setup, c3, Nbd2, and sometimes h3 or Ne5. Decision: Do not spend tempi on a KID maneuver designed for a center White never built. Warning: A universal setup becomes passive when it ignores the missing central pawn. Memory: Attack the center that exists, not the one you expected. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "12.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Torre with Bg5"]

{ Volume 12: Anti-King's Indian Systems. The early bishop pin aims for simple development and e4, so Black occupies or attacks the center before White completes the plan. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bg5 Bg7 4. Nbd2 d5 5. e3 O-O 6. Be2 c5 { Black plan: Use ...d5 and ...c5 to gain direct central play and question the bishop's role. White plan: White prepares c3, Bd3, and e4. Decision: Choose a central structure that makes Bg5 less relevant. Warning: Playing ...d6 and waiting can concede a comfortable version of White's system. Memory: When White develops modestly, claim the center modesty leaves available. } *

