[Result "*"]
[Variation "The f4 wedge"]

{ Volume 14: King's Indian Tactical Patterns. The pawn on f4 gains space, restricts g3 and e3, and prepares piece entry, but it also closes the f-file until contact is created. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. f3 f4 { Black plan: Bring pieces toward g4, h4, and f4 before opening the structure. White plan: White may play g4, h3, or c5 and race on the other wing. Decision: Decide whether ...g5, ...g4, or a piece maneuver is the next forcing step. Warning: Advancing the pawn wall without pieces creates targets instead of threats. Memory: The wedge restricts; another move must open access. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "14.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The g4 contact"]

{ Volume 14: King's Indian Tactical Patterns. The g-pawns create a hook, and ...h5 can force a file or fix White's shelter for a later sacrifice. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 13. Be1 g5 14. g4 Ng6 15. h3 h5 { Black plan: Calculate captures on g4 and h4 with queen and rook routes included. White plan: White tries to keep the kingside closed while breaking with c5. Decision: Count effective attackers and defenders before every pawn sacrifice. Warning: A familiar attacking shape does not prove a sacrifice. Memory: Pattern recognition finds the move; calculation earns it. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "14.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The counterblow during the race"]

{ Volume 14: King's Indian Tactical Patterns. Both sides have advanced, so a forcing move on either wing can make the other plan irrelevant. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. b4 g5 13. c5 Ng6 { Black plan: Search checks, captures, and direct threats before continuing the pawn script. White plan: White may break cxd6, Nb5, or b5 while Black attacks the king. Decision: Recalculate from the actual board after every pawn contact. Warning: Continuing a memorized attack after the queenside opens can lose material with no mating compensation. Memory: Every contact resets the calculation tree. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "15.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The e7-d7-f6 knight circuit"]

{ Volume 15: Piece Maneuvers and Strategic Coordination. The knight's retreat clears f7-f5, supports c5 and f4 from d7, then returns to f6 after the pawn has advanced. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 { Black plan: Time the route against White's queenside break and preserve useful squares for the other knight. White plan: White gains tempi for c5, b4, and pressure on d6. Decision: Ask what each knight move enables immediately. Warning: A thematic circuit played too slowly becomes pure loss of time. Memory: A maneuver is a sequence of jobs, not a tour of squares. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "15.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The dark bishop after the center closes"]

{ Volume 15: Piece Maneuvers and Strategic Coordination. The g7-bishop is blocked by e5 and d4, but it defends the king, pressures the center indirectly, and may awaken after captures. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 { Black plan: Keep the bishop unless an exchange removes a key defender or opens decisive dark squares. White plan: White may seek c5 and cxd6, changing the long diagonal. Decision: Forecast which central exchange will free the bishop. Warning: Calling the bishop bad can lead to an unnecessary trade that weakens the king. Memory: A blocked bishop may be a sleeping tactical piece and an active defender. } *

