[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Queen and rook entry routes"]

{ Volume 15: Piece Maneuvers and Strategic Coordination. Once pawn contact is prepared, the queen may enter through e8, g6, or h4 while rooks use f7-f8 or g-file openings. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 13. Be1 g5 14. g4 Ng6 15. h3 h5 { Black plan: Choose an entry route based on the file that will open, not on a memorized diagram. White plan: White may trade queens, close lines, or counter in the center. Decision: Before moving the queen, identify the opponent's forcing reply and the queen's retreat. Warning: A queen placed early can be chased while the queenside collapses. Memory: Heavy pieces belong behind the line that will actually open. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "16.1"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Meeting the Bayonet pressure"]

{ Volume 16: Defense, Resilience, and Counterplay. Black combines kingside activity with a timely central challenge so White cannot expand without calculation. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 10. Re1 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. Bf3 c6 { Black plan: Use ...c6 to question d5 and maintain enough queenside defense to keep the attack credible. White plan: White wants c5, a4, and b5 while exploiting the h5-knight. Decision: If the kingside attack is not forcing, spend a tempo reducing White's strongest break. Warning: Refusing all defensive moves is not bravery; it is loss of control. Memory: Counterattack works only while the position survives. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "16.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Neutralizing c5 with capture"]

{ Volume 16: Defense, Resilience, and Counterplay. Black can sometimes meet c5 by exchanging the advanced pawn and simplifying White's queenside initiative. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. c5 dxc5 12. Nxc5 Nxc5 { Black plan: Calculate the resulting piece activity and whether the d6 weakness disappears or becomes worse. White plan: White uses the recapture to activate a knight and open files. Decision: Choose capture only if the resulting trade reduces White's force without killing your own play. Warning: Automatic closure of the queenside can leave a permanent cramp. Memory: Defense is a favorable transformation, not merely a blocked file. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "16.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Pausing the pawn storm"]

{ Volume 16: Defense, Resilience, and Counterplay. White's c5 break may become more urgent than another kingside pawn push, requiring Black to improve a piece or defend a target. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. Bd2 Nf6 12. f3 f4 13. Be1 g5 14. c5 { Black plan: Preserve attacking potential while answering the concrete queenside threat. White plan: White attacks d6 and c7 and may use Nb5. Decision: Compare a forcing attack move with one consolidating move before deciding. Warning: Playing the "most King's Indian" move can be less accurate than playing the position. Memory: Identity never outranks necessity. } *

