[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The open-center queen trade"]

{ Volume 17: Exchanges, Endgames, and Transformations. When White releases the center, a queen trade can remove attacking chances and expose development and pawn-endgame details. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 { Black plan: Activate the rook on d8, develop quickly, and judge e4 as a target rather than chasing a vanished kingside attack. White plan: White seeks simplification, Be3, and control of the d-file. Decision: Count king activity and central weaknesses before welcoming the queen trade. Warning: Assuming every queenless KID favors the side with more space can miss Black's active pieces. Memory: After queens leave, replace attack language with activity language. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "17.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The fianchetto central exchange"]

{ Volume 17: Exchanges, Endgames, and Transformations. The exchange on d4 transforms space into piece pressure and often produces a strategic middlegame that may simplify further. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. Nc3 e5 8. e4 c6 9. h3 exd4 10. Nxd4 Re8 11. Re1 Nc5 { Black plan: Use active rooks and knights, pressure e4, and avoid a passive defense of d6. White plan: White seeks Be3, Qd2, and exchanges that preserve the better bishop. Decision: Forecast which minor piece you want in the ending before initiating trades. Warning: Trading all active pieces can leave Black with only weaknesses. Memory: Simplify the opponent's activity, not your own. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "17.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Saemisch structural liquidation"]

{ Volume 17: Exchanges, Endgames, and Transformations. Central exchanges leave White with space and Black with clear targets and queenside breaks. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 e5 7. d5 c6 8. Qd2 cxd5 9. cxd5 a6 { Black plan: Use ...b5 and piece pressure to force White to defend the advanced center. White plan: White may castle long and use the open c-file or kingside pawns. Decision: Judge the ending by pawn targets, bishop scope, and king routes, not by space alone. Warning: Entering an ending with a permanently weak d6 pawn requires active compensation. Memory: A good transformation leaves a target you can reach. } *

