[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "Classical Pirc boundary"]

{ Volume 18: Pirc Bridge and Lifetime Repertoire System. The piece shell resembles the KID, but White's c-pawn is uncommitted and the center can open faster, so Black cannot assume a closed-wing race. } 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Be2 O-O { Black plan: Challenge with ...e5 or ...c6 and keep the queen and king ready for direct central tactics. White plan: White may castle quickly, play Re1, and use e5 or h3. Decision: Count the central pawns and ask how quickly the e- and d-files can open. Warning: Importing automatic ...f5 attacks from the KID can expose Black before development is complete. Memory: Same bishop, different center, different opening. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "18.2"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The Austrian Attack"]

{ Volume 18: Pirc Bridge and Lifetime Repertoire System. White uses the f-pawn immediately, creating more central force and more dark-square and king-safety responsibility. } 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O { Black plan: Strike the center with ...c5 or ...e5 and calculate e4-e5 before slow moves. White plan: White wants e5, Be3, Qd2, and a direct kingside attack. Decision: Treat every central break as a tactical event and verify the king's escape squares. Warning: Passive KID-style maneuvering lets White's broad center attack before it becomes a target. Memory: In the Pirc, the center often must be hit before it closes. } *

[Event "KID Deep Understanding Course"]
[Site "Study file"]
[Date "2026.09.02"]
[Round "18.3"]
[White "Reference line"]
[Black "Black repertoire"]
[Result "*"]
[Variation "The 150 Attack and study system"]

{ Volume 18: Pirc Bridge and Lifetime Repertoire System. White aims for Bh6, long castling, and h-pawn contact, so Black builds queenside counterplay and a retreat square for the queen. } 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Be3 Bg7 5. Qd2 c6 { Black plan: Use ...b5, ...Nbd7, and central breaks while tracking Bh6 and e5. White plan: White seeks a direct opposite-wing attack. Decision: Maintain separate KID and Pirc files, but tag shared patterns such as fianchetto defense, central breaks, and race counting. Warning: Combining the repertoires into one move tree hides the decisive difference made by White's c-pawn. Memory: Transfer questions and patterns; do not transfer moves blindly. } *

